['Certainly! Below is a simple C program that demonstrates how to use `argc` and `argv` to handle command-line arguments, including converting them to integers using `atoi()`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s number1 number2 ...\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    // Convert command-line arguments to integers and print them
    for (int i = 1; i < argc; ++i) {
        int num = atoi(argv[i]);
        printf("Argument %d: %s -> Integer: %d\n", i, argv[i], num);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Explanation:
- `argc` is the number of arguments passed to the program. It includes the name of the program itself.
- `argv` is an array of strings where each string is one of the arguments passed to the program.
- The first argument, `argv[0]`, is always the name of the program.
- The loop starting from `i = 1` iterates over each command-line argument (excluding the program name).
- `atoi(argv[i])` converts the string at `argv[i]` to an integer.

### Example Usage:
If you compile and run this program with the following command:

```sh
gcc -o myprogram myprogram.c
./myprogram 123 456 789
```

The output will be:

```
Argument 1: 123 -> Integer: 123
Argument 2: 456 -> Integer: 456
Argument 3: 789 -> Integer: 789
```

This demonstrates how to read and convert command-line arguments in a C program.']

